  <section|Proof audit><label|proof-audit>

  <verbatim|Store_l> packages the outputs of <verbatim|july> and
  <verbatim|year_spec> with the already selected scaling, hierarchy, and
  same-history chart. The first theorem is not a new selection: it obtains
  <verbatim|ChoiceK> from <verbatim|reveals>. <verbatim|reveals> uses the
  three source-provided paid tensor families (principal, false correction,
  true correction), obtains the same-history response, and calls
  <verbatim|formulate> to solve the two-direction cancellation.
  <verbatim|formulate> proves its matrix eventually bijective by convergence
  to the invertible reference two-direction map, obtains a bounded solution
  by finite-dimensional stable inversion, and uses
  <verbatim|transferX>/<verbatim|mere> to calibrate the second coefficient.
  The prose gives the exact finite-dimensional inversion and coefficient
  extraction. Its calibration uses the displayed principal-response inputs,
  the weighted response comparison <verbatim|gapC>, the Ward comparison
  <verbatim|diadem>, and the paid sensor estimate <verbatim|Prosper.vote>;
  the sensor estimate is an incoming producer boundary for this unit.
  Likewise the branch calculation reduces the field and trace limits to the
  operator convergence of <verbatim|Moment4>, the finite branch coefficient
  limits, the exponent-one <verbatim|muteP> bounds, and the true-branch trace
  identity <verbatim|sum_delta>. The latter branch limits are the other
  incoming producer boundary, not consequences of unweighted field
  convergence alone. <verbatim|serene> checks conservation from the exact
  cancellation equation.

  For <verbatim|year_spec>, the physical tensor and the correction sequence
  belong to the same one-dimensional conserved kernel. <verbatim|awake_o>
  finds a nonzero reference radial derivative and an observer test.
  <verbatim|pay_jet> and <verbatim|comparison_jets> give convergence of the
  identical shell derivative for both sequences. If the physical tensor were
  zero, its measured sequence would vanish, contradicting the nonzero
  reference limit. Rank one therefore gives a scalar ratio; evaluation by a
  linear functional nonzero on the reference limit proves that ratio tends to
  one. The field and flux limits then transfer by homogeneity. This is why
  the shell test is essential: the rank-one statement alone leaves the scalar
  undetermined.

  <verbatim|july> uses <verbatim|center_plus> to write the comparison trace
  as the trace of the recentered physical stress plus a scalar multiple of
  the unit. <verbatim|year_spec> handles the first term;
  <verbatim|shift_unit> makes the second term vanish at the zoomed scale.
  <verbatim|trace_Year> identifies the zoomed trace with the diagonal trace
  of the normalized physical stress. <verbatim|leaderB> applies a left
  inverse of the scalar embedding and takes the two coordinates, yielding the
  exact eventual decomposition and its two limits. <verbatim|Store_l> then
  fills the Scribe fields directly; <verbatim|gang> uses <verbatim|pays_sky>
  and <verbatim|gravity> to obtain a selected scaling and chart for the
  arbitrary-null-sequence clause before invoking the same <verbatim|Store_l>
  at that same history.

  The prose reuses the reviewed units <verbatim|sec:ex-lattice-trace-producer>,
  <verbatim|sec:ex-lattice-trace-weighted-estimates>,
  <verbatim|sec:ex-lattice-trace-crown-buffer>, and
  <verbatim|sec:ex-lattice-trace-eve-producer> for the numerical source
  prices and scale comparisons. It does not reproduce their kernel-rank,
  response-price, or finite-probe calculations. The physical stress transfer
  above is the additional same-history bridge required by the
  scale/tested-chart argument in <verbatim|sec:ex-lattice-firesky-gallery>.

  <section|Commands and remaining audit><label|commands-and-remaining-audit>

  Read-only inspection used <verbatim|rg -n> and <verbatim|sed -n> on the
  listed declarations and their immediate proof files, including the full
  <verbatim|formulate>, <verbatim|transferX>, <verbatim|mere>,
  <verbatim|pairingD>, <verbatim|beater>, <verbatim|gapC>,
  <verbatim|penumbra>, <verbatim|diadem>, <verbatim|Moment4>,
  <verbatim|unveil>, <verbatim|nearing_h>, <verbatim|muteP>,
  <verbatim|blossoms>, <verbatim|year_spec>, and <verbatim|Store_l> proofs. I
  ran <verbatim|python3 scripts/check_blueprint.py>; it stopped because the
  isolated clone does not contain the coordinator-owned
  <verbatim|blueprint/units/YM-000026/unit.json>. No Lean build or LaTeX
  build was run.

  The remaining producer boundaries are precise. The paid sensor package in
  <verbatim|YM.Trace.Terrace.Prosper.vote> and its construction from the
  terrace sensor receipts are assigned separately. The finite branch labels,
  operator limit, and exponent-one principal/false trace bounds used from
  <verbatim|YM.Trace.Shelves.nearing_h> and
  <verbatim|YM.Trace.Shelves.muteP>, together with the true-branch trace
  identity, are also assigned separately. This chapter proves the stable
  inversion, weighted response decomposition, finite field/trace assembly,
  nonzero-shell proportionality, and scalar-coordinate extraction from those
  inputs. The public arbitrary-null-sequence selection is treated in
  <verbatim|sec:ex-lattice-firesky-gallery>; this unit reuses that route and
  does not replace its scale-selection proof.

